A thread holds its process by a node that is a root rather than one below the Process capability,
so revoking that capability, or destroying the process's pool, does not stop the thread,
which would go on in a process that is gone;
a thread's process lies below its pool's own node.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: capability to an object is a root

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -333,7 +333,7 @@ static int op_pool(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
         }
         /* It hangs below the capability it was made with, so a revoke above that stops the thread. */
         nt->proc = (struct cap){ .type = CAP_HOSTED, .rights = pc.rights, .a = pc.a };
-        cap_attach(slot_node(t, arg[3]), &nt->proc);
+        cap_attach(NULL, &nt->proc);
         nt->state = THREAD_STOPPED;
         /* Until it is configured while tracing is off, the host cannot follow it. */
         nt->flags = THREAD_UNTRACED;
